# Copy to .env and set the path for this machine.
#
# Compose reads .env automatically when run from this directory. Without it,
# ${OPENCODE_GLOBAL_CONFIG} is empty and the server/discover/eval volume
# specs collapse to ":/home/harness/.config/opencode/opencode.json:ro",
# which compose rejects with "empty section between colons".
#
# The Makefile defaults this with ?= so shell invocations work regardless.
# Anything driving compose directly -- an orchestrator, a CI runner, a bare
# `docker compose` call -- gets no such default and needs this file.
#
# Must be an absolute path to your real global opencode config, which is
# mounted read-only as the harness's provider and auth source.
OPENCODE_GLOBAL_CONFIG=/home/YOUR_USER/.config/opencode/opencode.json

# The uid:gid the containers run as. Everything written to ./results and
# ./notebooks lands with this ownership on the host; without it the
# containers run as root and leave files you cannot edit or delete
# without sudo. `id -u` and `id -g` give the right values.
#
# The Makefile derives both itself, so `make server-up` and friends need
# nothing here. Anything calling compose directly does, exactly as with
# OPENCODE_GLOBAL_CONFIG above -- absent, the compose file falls back to
# 1000:1000, which is right on a single-user machine and wrong anywhere
# else.
HOST_UID=1000
HOST_GID=1000

# This directory's absolute path on the host, used as the prefix for
# every bind-mount source in docker-compose.yml.
#
# Leave it unset for shell use: the compose file defaults it to `.`, and
# compose resolves that against the project directory, which is right
# whenever you are running in this directory.
#
# It is REQUIRED when something else drives compose from a different
# filesystem view -- cicd_runner's coordinator being the case that
# proved it. There, `.` resolved to a path that exists only inside that
# container, the daemon created an empty directory tree at the host root
# rather than erroring, and the server crash-looped on missing
# credentials.
#
# Get the value with pwd, from this directory:
#
#     HARNESS_ROOT=$(pwd)
#
# Not `realpath` and not `readlink -f`. The same paths are resolved by
# the auth extraction scripts and by Terraform's abspath(), neither of
# which resolves symlinks -- a value that disagrees with them describes
# the same file by a different string, which Terraform reads as drift
# and acts on by recreating the container.
# HARNESS_ROOT=/home/YOUR_USER/path/to/opencode-model-eval
